---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Penguin-Layout.astro';
import Navbar from '../../components/Navbar.astro';

const currentDate = new Date();
let currentYear = currentDate.getFullYear();
---

<Layout title="Penguins">
	<head>
		<meta charset="UTF-8">
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
		<title>Adelie Penguins</title>
	</head>
	<body>
		<header class="header">
        	<h1>Adelie Penguin</h1>
    	</header>
		<Navbar/>
        <main>
            <div class="container">
                <nav class="contents">
                    <h2>Contents:</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#diet">Diet</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#behavior">Social Behavior</a></li>
                        <li><a href="#facts">Facts</a></li>
                    </ul>
                </nav>
                
                <section id="overview">
                    <h2>Overview</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Scientific Name: Pygoscelis adeliae</li>
                        <li>Size: Medium-sized penguins, standing 70 to 73 cm (28 to 29 inches) tall and weighing 3.8 to 8.2 kg (8.4 to 18.1 lb).</li>
                        <li>Habitat: Breed on rocky coasts of Antarctica and surrounding islands.</li>
                        <li>Conservation Status: Near Threatened (IUCN)</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
                <section id="diet">
                    <h2>Diet</h2>
                    <p>Adelie penguins are opportunistic eaters, primarily feeding on krill, small crustaceans found in abundance in the Southern Ocean. They also consume fish, squid, and other marine invertebrates.  These birds are excellent swimmers, using their flippers to propel themselves through the water at speeds of up to 7 km/h (4 mph).</p>
                </section>
                <section id="behavior">
                    <h2>Social Behavior</h2>
                    <p>Adelie penguins are highly social creatures, forming large breeding colonies that can number in the tens of thousands.  During breeding season, males build pebble nests where females lay two eggs.  Both parents share incubation duties and chick rearing, a behavior uncommon among birds.  Adelie penguins are known for their vocalizations, using a variety of calls to communicate with each other.</p>
                </section>
                <section id="facts">
                    <h2>Interesting Facts</h2>
                    <ul>
                        <li>Adelie penguins are named after Adélie Land, which was itself named after Adèle Dumont d'Urville, the wife of a French explorer.</li>
                        <li>These penguins are known for their aggressive behavior, sometimes even defending themselves against much larger predators.</li>
                        <li>Adelie penguins are an important indicator species for the health of the Antarctic ecosystem. Changes in their population can signal problems in the food chain or the environment.</li>
                    </ul>
                </section>
            </div>
            <footer>
                <p><span class="copyleft">&copy;</span> PengiCoder & MrPiggy105, { currentYear }</p>
            </footer>
        </main>
	</body>
</Layout>

<style>
    .contents ul {
        list-style-type: none;
        padding: 0;
    }

    .contents li {
        margin-bottom: 10px;
    }

    section {
        overflow: hidden;
        margin-bottom: 20px;
        margin-left: 30%;
    }

    h1{
        text-align: center;
        height: 100%;
        font-family: 'Orkney', Arial;
    }
</style>
